# Full-Stack Defaults

- TypeScript-first with strict typing and shared contract types.
- Define API request/response contracts before implementing handlers/components.
- Use Zod validation at trust boundaries.
- Enforce auth and authorization across DB, API middleware, and UI entrypoints.

# Next.js and Rendering

- This is not legacy Next.js; verify behavior against `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`.
- Default to React Server Components; use client components only for required interactivity.
- Choose route strategy intentionally (RSC/SSR/ISR/static) by freshness requirement.
- Use Suspense-friendly streaming patterns for slow server reads.
- For edge runtime paths, avoid Node-only APIs and document runtime assumptions.

# API + Frontend Conventions

- Prefer shared route helpers for auth/capability checks and error shaping.
- Use stable machine-readable error codes over fragile string matching.
- Keep optimistic concurrency and stale-data handling explicit in clients.
- Avoid route proliferation; consolidate aliases and remove dead routes quickly.

# Architecture and Ops Expectations

- Treat real-time features as first-class architecture: define reconnect and conflict behavior.
- Keep performance explicit: query efficiency, bundle impact, and cache strategy should be reviewed.
- Keep security explicit: least-privilege auth, RBAC/RLS-aware flows, no secret leakage to clients.
- For monorepo/package changes, keep shared types/contracts in one source of truth.
- Keep delivery atomic: migrations + API + UI ship together with rollback clarity.
